Dialogue: 0,0:13:06.39,0:13:09.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK good afternoon, everybody.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:09.33,0:13:12.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,Thanks for coming.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:12.75,0:13:20.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is the first of the first colloquium of the colloquium of the semester.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:20.51,0:13:21.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so welcome everybody.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:21.97,0:13:31.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,We are here every Wednesday at this time, and we get quite a variety of topics and speakers.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:31.27,0:13:35.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,Generally we get very, very prominent high quality speakers.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:35.63,0:13:38.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,And today we start out with.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:41.23,0:13:46.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,A very, very good first case.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:46.12,0:13:48.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,We actually have two speakers.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:48.86,0:13:51.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,We have Tony yen.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:51.18,0:13:55.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,He's the vice president head of technology development at ASML.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:55.08,0:14:03.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,I assume all of you who are here have heard of ASML and know the key role they play in the semiconductor industry.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:03.82,0:14:18.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,And Tony is currently in addition to his continuing in his role as VP and Head of technology development at ASML, he is also serving as a visiting professor in our department.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:18.50,0:14:23.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,He will be teaching a course in the.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:23.66,0:14:25.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,In the fall.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:25.54,0:14:29.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,Sorry spring semester on the topic of today's talk.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:29.28,0:14:34.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,So if you're interested in this material, sign up for Tony's class.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:34.64,0:14:42.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,Tony was he got his bachelor's degree from Purdue, his PhD and MBA from MIT.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:42.50,0:14:50.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,He was. He worked at TI and at TSMC for quite a while before joining ASML.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:50.40,0:14:55.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,He's a fellow of IEEE and won a number of important awards.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:55.96,0:15:01.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,Tony's going to start out today, but he has a colleague here, Ron Gosens,
Dialogue: 0,0:15:01.10,0:15:12.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,also Gosens, also from ASML, who is a senior strategy manager at ASML.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:12.42,0:15:13.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,He's working part time.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:13.82,0:15:16.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,He's partly retired.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:16.92,0:15:22.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,He is also an adjunct professor at Purdue, where he teaches,
Dialogue: 0,0:15:22.08,0:15:31.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,and he got educated in the Netherlands, at Utrecht and has been at Philips research, Stanford national, NXP
Dialogue: 0,0:15:31.52,0:15:36.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,before joining ASML, where he leads computational lithography efforts.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:36.48,0:15:46.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,So they're going to do a tag team and tell us about EUV lithography, which is the hot thing now in microelectronics.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:46.69,0:15:49.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,And it'll give us the history and where we are and where we're going.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:49.89,0:15:52.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I'll start with Tony.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:55.91,0:15:58.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,Thank you.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:58.11,0:16:00.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,Thank you, Jeff, for this very nice introduction.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:00.49,0:16:01.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,Is the microphone on.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:01.87,0:16:03.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yes thank you.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:03.11,0:16:10.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,And Ronald and I were very happy to be here at Berkeley to give this colloquium.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:10.01,0:16:15.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,And we without further ado, let me get started.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:15.27,0:16:17.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,Perhaps I'll get a little excited.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:17.25,0:16:20.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,So let me remove my jacket first, just in case.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:20.24,0:16:22.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,Don't think you want to stand in the middle because you'll walk.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:22.91,0:16:25.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,No, no, I will be standing here.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:25.63,0:16:33.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,So we want to tell you about EUV lithography.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:33.07,0:16:37.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,But I want to start with some history.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:37.07,0:16:40.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,But I'd like to start with the history of photolithography itself.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:40.43,0:16:42.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,Just give me five minutes.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:42.11,0:16:53.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,Because this may be interesting to know if some of you do not know photolithography or are not familiar with this technology yet, but.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:53.23,0:17:01.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,So let me get started, it's the first part of the talk is the photolithography until early 1980s and then EUV as a next
Dialogue: 0,0:17:01.97,0:17:10.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,generation technology, lithography technology, and then developing EUV lithography for high volume production.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:10.17,0:17:17.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,So photolithography itself was invented in the mid 1950s by two groups.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:17.65,0:17:21.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,First was called Bell Labs where you folks were.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:21.85,0:17:25.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the second group was a US Army's laboratory.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:25.29,0:17:31.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,So let me focus on the second group because the story was more dramatic.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:31.25,0:17:38.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,In the second group, the goal was to fit a proximity fuse diffuse into a mortar shell.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:38.97,0:17:40.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,Mortar shells are not very big.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:40.59,0:17:44.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,A proximity fuse is basically a cheap radar system.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:44.27,0:17:52.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK, the goal is to have the bomb explode in the proximity of the target before hitting the target.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:52.79,0:17:56.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,To increase the probability of impact.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:56.11,0:18:00.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,Because if it explodes right on target, you may miss the target.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:00.01,0:18:04.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,But if you explore the vicinity and your chances are a lot higher hitting the target.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:04.37,0:18:09.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,So this was the goal to put the proximity fuse inside a mortar shell.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:09.05,0:18:10.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK mortar shell is very small.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:10.99,0:18:16.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,So in those days the transistors were mesa transistors.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:16.53,0:18:18.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,Germanium OK.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:18.09,0:18:23.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,How do you fit a mesa germanium transistor circuit inside a mortar shell.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:23.73,0:18:25.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, these two guys.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:27.91,0:18:32.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,Jay Lathrop and James Knoll from the laboratory,
Dialogue: 0,0:18:32.57,0:18:43.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,they thought of using, of course, they found out that Kodak had a photoresist and they thought of using a microscope, but operating in reverse.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:43.94,0:18:46.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,So this is a microscope.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:46.12,0:18:49.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,And this is where the transistor or a piece of germanium is.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:49.82,0:18:54.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,And they put the photomask here where you would have the Polaroid camera.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:54.08,0:18:57.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,And you shine white light from the top.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:57.26,0:18:58.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now what is this filter.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:58.80,0:19:07.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, before the exposure, when you're aligning to the previous layer, you slide the red filter in there so that the photoresist is not exposed.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:07.54,0:19:12.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,Once you do the alignment, you take out the red filter and then the exposure happens.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:12.50,0:19:16.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,You expose this and then you get the transistor.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:16.54,0:19:18.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the transistor looked like this.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:18.64,0:19:23.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,The die is about 1 millimeter to 1 millimeter by 1 millimeter.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:23.36,0:19:24.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,And this is a mesa.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:24.36,0:19:25.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,The base size was not stated.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:25.96,0:19:27.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,The base contact.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:27.26,0:19:34.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,This little guy, one of these guys is a base contact is 0.1 by 0.25.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:34.82,0:19:36.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,Nowadays it's gigantic.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:36.50,0:19:38.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,But back in those days, it was hard to make.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:38.56,0:19:43.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then they also had to make the emitter contact, which is the other rectangle right here.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:43.66,0:19:46.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,Same Same dimension.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:46.08,0:19:47.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,It was a success.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:47.72,0:19:53.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then what happened was that Jay Lathrop joined ti in 1958.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:53.68,0:19:56.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,And what happened in TI at 19.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:56.02,0:20:00.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,In 1958, Jack Kilby invented the integrated circuit.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:00.78,0:20:06.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,So Jay Lathrop worked at TI with Jack Kilby for the next 10 years.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:06.92,0:20:10.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,And where did the other guy go.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:10.04,0:20:12.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,Of course, he went to Fairchild.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:12.60,0:20:21.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,1959 right where Robert Noyce and others had invented the integrated circuit there.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:21.00,0:20:25.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,So great story, and it's a bit romantic.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:25.24,0:20:29.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,So this was in the mid to late 1950s.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:29.08,0:20:31.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,And what happened next.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:31.04,0:20:36.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,What happened next is less romantic lithography.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:36.33,0:20:44.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,In the 60s and the 70, early 70s were mainly contact and proximity printing.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:44.83,0:20:50.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,Because those days, resolution wasn't really the top issue.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:50.25,0:20:53.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,The top issue was how to get these layers to align.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:53.25,0:20:57.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,So those machines were called aligners back then.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:57.35,0:21:03.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,And this is an aligner from Casper instruments here in the US.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:03.91,0:21:08.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,These guys were the King back in the 60s OK.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:08.59,0:21:11.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,They were very good at doing contact printing.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:11.47,0:21:13.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,We put the mask right on top of the wafer.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:13.69,0:21:14.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,You're aligned.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:14.57,0:21:16.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then you shine the light.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:16.35,0:21:23.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,The problem with contact printing is that it generates particles in the space or.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:23.07,0:21:26.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, you're rubbing the wafer against the mask.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:26.51,0:21:29.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,So what happened was that later.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:29.15,0:21:34.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, maybe we should create a gap between the mask and the wafer.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:34.15,0:21:38.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then they went from contact printing to proximity printing.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:38.29,0:21:44.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,Once proximity printing happens, canon took over as the new King.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:44.01,0:21:46.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,This guy faded away.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:46.35,0:21:52.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then canon made a much better machine for proximity printing.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:52.29,0:21:57.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,So this is canon machine until.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:57.37,0:22:03.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, until proximity printing was superseded by projection imaging.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:03.13,0:22:06.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,And this happened in the early 70s.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:06.61,0:22:09.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,So let me first give an example.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:09.49,0:22:12.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,Why did we go for projection imaging.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:12.45,0:22:23.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,So the example is let's shine a lambda equal to 400 nanometer UV light through a mask.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:23.73,0:22:28.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,Imaging a slit slit is 4 micron wide slit right.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:28.51,0:22:33.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the gap is 1 micron 5 micron and 10 microns.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:33.75,0:22:37.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,And this is the kind of aerial image that you get.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:37.01,0:22:45.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,As you can see that this is almost at the limit of resolution Of proximity printing.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:45.47,0:22:46.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,Because why.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:46.33,0:22:48.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,Because you really cannot control the gap very well.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:48.69,0:22:51.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,If you control the gap perfectly then it's fine.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:51.91,0:22:57.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,But you can't because first the control itself mechanism itself wasn't very good.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:57.09,0:23:01.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,But also the wafer has is not flat.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:01.99,0:23:03.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's warped.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:03.07,0:23:08.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,So it's much better if you do it through a lens.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:08.03,0:23:15.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,And this is projection printing same wavelengths of light with a lens that is not very sophisticated.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:15.89,0:23:19.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,0.2 a very low numerical aperture lens.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:19.39,0:23:21.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,And this is what you get if you're unfocused.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:21.89,0:23:23.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,You're looking at the red curve.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:23.41,0:23:29.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,And if you're off by plus 5 plus minus 5 microns you're looking at the blue curve.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:29.78,0:23:34.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,So in one case you're fighting diffraction.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:34.98,0:23:38.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,The other case, you're taking advantage of diffraction.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:38.42,0:23:41.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,Of course, with the addition of a lens.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:41.30,0:23:45.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,So this is what's going on in terms of physics.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:45.82,0:23:55.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then of course, early 1970s, there was this company called perkin-elmer.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:55.90,0:24:04.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,They made the first commercially successful projection aligner, to be precise, in 1973.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:04.30,0:24:12.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,This, this was the perkin-elmer microline 1X, meaning no magnification or magnification.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:12.08,0:24:15.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,Reflective imaging system with an annular slit.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:15.64,0:24:17.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's actually quite clever.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:17.12,0:24:24.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,The designer of this optics was called Abe offner, a gigantic figure in lens design.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:24.82,0:24:26.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,Sorry he passed away.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:26.92,0:24:30.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,But this system has only two mirrors.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:30.70,0:24:35.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,Concentric concentric, two mirrors, one concave, one convex.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:35.58,0:24:40.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the imaging is done through a slit y slit.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:40.70,0:24:52.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,Because Abe figured out that the optimal distance from the optical axis is this thing called H0 at H0 from the optical axis.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:52.12,0:24:58.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,The aberration was minimized through a slit is fine because you can scan the whole thing,
Dialogue: 0,0:24:58.98,0:25:06.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,because the mask and the wafer are actually ganged together through a shaft, which is not shown down here.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:06.50,0:25:09.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,So the exposure happens after alignment.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:09.62,0:25:11.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,You basically do this right.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:11.62,0:25:16.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,You can never go wrong because they're together in one axis OK.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:16.04,0:25:19.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,And this was the first of course.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:19.62,0:25:21.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,Then it became a hit.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:21.88,0:25:34.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,Thousands of these machines were sold and the first one went to Texas Instruments for a price of 90,000 $95,000.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:34.99,0:25:38.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,It cannot even get a mask with this kind of money.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:38.63,0:25:40.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,Euv mask, of course.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:40.11,0:25:42.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,So this was sold to TI.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:42.51,0:25:44.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then what happened.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:44.23,0:25:46.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,Intel 8088.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:46.31,0:25:47.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK, this was not ti.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:47.53,0:25:49.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,This was Intel.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:49.03,0:25:56.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,A device size was 0.2 by point is about 5 millimeters by 5 millimeter device size.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:56.37,0:25:57.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,With contact printing.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:57.53,0:26:07.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,The yield of these microprocessors were only 20% with micro microline 60% This is basically it.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:07.91,0:26:11.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,I mean, Moore's law alive back then.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:11.79,0:26:16.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,And now, of course, this wasn't the end of the story.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:16.65,0:26:24.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,They were the King for a few years until the King is dead and long live the King.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:24.41,0:26:26.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,The new King, of course.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:26.65,0:26:28.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,Who's the new King.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:28.65,0:26:36.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, the problem with the one x design was that it was a simple optical system.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:36.29,0:26:38.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,Two mirrors, all spherical.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:38.47,0:26:40.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,Both spherical.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:40.29,0:26:43.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,So the could not be made very high.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:43.65,0:26:48.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,The numerical aperture of the microline was 0.167.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:48.43,0:26:49.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,Very low.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:49.37,0:26:54.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,In order to get better resolution, you need to increase the numerical aperture.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:54.65,0:27:00.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,However, in order to do so on the offner system, the aberration would become very high.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:00.85,0:27:13.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,So we had to get away from this kind of a offner design and to come back to the more traditional D magnified lens design.
