Dialogue: 0,0:54:27.45,0:54:31.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,So in general try to go to a shorter wavelength.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:31.45,0:54:39.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,That's always very complicated to go from 193 to 13.5 what was the timeline.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:39.37,0:54:42.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,How many years did you say 35 years.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:42.27,0:54:47.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,So we're going to stick with 13.5 nanometers for quite a while.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:47.07,0:54:49.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,The next thing that you can do is go to a larger.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:49.59,0:54:51.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,But you already saw how expensive that is.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:51.65,0:54:54.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then you have the third factor, the K1.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:54.83,0:55:00.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,And I kind of feel a little bit pretentious being here,
Dialogue: 0,0:55:00.85,0:55:09.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,because then we start talking about computational lithography and who am I daring to show up here at Berkeley, where
Dialogue: 0,0:55:09.11,0:55:15.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,one of your professors, Andy neureuter he's really the godfather of computational lithography.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:15.21,0:55:18.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I'm basically preaching to the choir.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:18.75,0:55:23.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,But allow me anyway to share my view on some of this.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:23.31,0:55:34.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,Obviously, we stand on the shoulders of many Giants that have helped with developing a lot of the theory all the way from Fresnel, Fourier, Maxwell, Helmholtz, Ernst
Dialogue: 0,0:55:34.75,0:55:46.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,Abbe there, this guy here that was one of the first chief scientists of Zeiss, did a lot of work on microscopy and developed it from there.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:49.13,0:55:49.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,What did he say.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:49.95,0:55:52.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,He say in 1873 already.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:52.87,0:56:02.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,Hey, the minimum pitch is 1/2 times the wavelength divided by the sine of that opening angle that we now call the.
Dialogue: 0,0:56:02.01,0:56:04.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,And he didn't have the refractive index yet.
Dialogue: 0,0:56:04.59,0:56:09.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now we say that n times the sine of that angle is the numerical aperture.
Dialogue: 0,0:56:09.53,0:56:12.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,But isn't it remarkable.
Dialogue: 0,0:56:12.81,0:56:25.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,Since 1873 to the day of today, a very simple formula has governed our roadmap, and the rest was simply technology.
Dialogue: 0,0:56:25.53,0:56:28.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, technology is not always simple.
Dialogue: 0,0:56:28.89,0:56:37.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,Lin from TSMC said, hey, lambda and Na, that's physics and that's geometry of your lenses.
Dialogue: 0,0:56:37.69,0:56:45.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,But that K1, that's all kind of illumination tricks chemistry, resist contrast, aberrations, and mask tricks.
Dialogue: 0,0:56:45.28,0:56:49.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the minimum value that you can accomplish is 0.25.
Dialogue: 0,0:56:49.32,0:56:57.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I'm going to talk about what can we all do to reduce that K1 if we want to do that with computation.
Dialogue: 0,0:56:57.20,0:57:01.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,It starts with I need to have a good model for what am I actually getting.
Dialogue: 0,0:57:01.22,0:57:12.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I need a forward model with which I can predict what is the quality of the imaging that I get and that good model that will involve a MAST model.
Dialogue: 0,0:57:12.96,0:57:16.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,I need to see what is happening in my optics.
Dialogue: 0,0:57:16.44,0:57:18.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,What is the reaction of my resist.
Dialogue: 0,0:57:18.62,0:57:20.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,That's a lot of chemistry.
Dialogue: 0,0:57:20.04,0:57:23.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,And ultimately, I don't care what the pattern is, am I resist.
Dialogue: 0,0:57:23.34,0:57:25.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,I want it to have it in a device layer.
Dialogue: 0,0:57:25.74,0:57:30.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I need an edge model that becomes my forward model.
Dialogue: 0,0:57:30.08,0:57:36.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,And a lot of effort has gone into how do I make that forward model as good as I can.
Dialogue: 0,0:57:36.84,0:57:39.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,And we continue to improve with that.
Dialogue: 0,0:57:39.22,0:57:41.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,More and more physics, more and more chemistry.
Dialogue: 0,0:57:41.76,0:57:45.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,And at some point in time, we said, hey, AI is starting to take off.
Dialogue: 0,0:57:45.76,0:57:48.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,And perhaps when we have a lot of good measurements,
Dialogue: 0,0:57:48.34,0:57:59.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,we can develop an AI model that can predict what I will get, even without fully understanding why that is what I will get and all of that is being employed.
Dialogue: 0,0:57:59.58,0:58:04.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,But once I have such a good model, I can go and play all kinds of tricks.
Dialogue: 0,0:58:04.18,0:58:13.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,I can make a little manufacturing check where with a little layer and a little model that I have developed, I can now say, what am I going to get on the wafer.
Dialogue: 0,0:58:13.88,0:58:17.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,And is that what I wanted in the first place.
Dialogue: 0,0:58:17.62,0:58:23.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,Another option is that I say, hey, I have a little layer and I have measurements on what is the wafer.
Dialogue: 0,0:58:23.60,0:58:28.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,I can calibrate that model or I have a little model.
Dialogue: 0,0:58:28.86,0:58:31.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,I know what I want on the wafer.
Dialogue: 0,0:58:31.22,0:58:37.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,What do I need to put on my reticle so that I get, in the end, what I wanted in the first place.
Dialogue: 0,0:58:37.18,0:58:40.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,And you contributed quite a bit of that.
Dialogue: 0,0:58:40.07,0:58:43.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,That's where the field of inverse lithography and so on.
Dialogue: 0,0:58:43.11,0:58:45.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,I'm going to talk about a different one today.
Dialogue: 0,0:58:45.23,0:58:46.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,And that's this one here.
Dialogue: 0,0:58:46.65,0:58:48.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,I know what I want on the wafer.
Dialogue: 0,0:58:48.51,0:58:50.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,How can I optimize.
Dialogue: 0,0:58:50.03,0:58:52.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,What is the pattern that I put on the little layer.
Dialogue: 0,0:58:52.73,0:58:56.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,And how do I instruct my scanner.
Dialogue: 0,0:58:56.07,0:59:04.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,What is the illumination that I will use on the scanner to maximize the contrast to get the best possible results.
Dialogue: 0,0:59:04.85,0:59:11.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now, when we talk about illumination, let me talk for a moment about an illumination system.
Dialogue: 0,0:59:11.45,0:59:18.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,Trick is what I would like to have is very, very uniform illumination of my mask.
Dialogue: 0,0:59:18.53,0:59:28.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,And in order to play with interference and to play with diffraction and be able to manipulate exactly what is the interference pattern
Dialogue: 0,0:59:28.33,0:59:35.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,that I get on the wafer, I need to make sure that this is spatially coherent light.
Dialogue: 0,0:59:35.61,0:59:37.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,And how do we do that.
Dialogue: 0,0:59:37.15,0:59:40.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,We create this so-called 4f system.
Dialogue: 0,0:59:40.19,0:59:45.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,A guy Kohler invented this by having this four lens system.
Dialogue: 0,0:59:45.99,0:59:55.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,What I can create is, for every point in the light source, there is one particular set of planar waves that hits the mask.
Dialogue: 0,0:59:55.23,1:00:04.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,So if I imagine myself sitting on the mask and I look back at the illumination, imagine a circle
Dialogue: 0,1:00:04.55,1:00:07.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,and I could get light from that whole circle at me.
Dialogue: 0,1:00:07.57,1:00:09.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,That would be a cone of light.
Dialogue: 0,1:00:09.75,1:00:13.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,I could have light just coming from the center of that point.
Dialogue: 0,1:00:13.25,1:00:15.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,That would be one planar wave.
Dialogue: 0,1:00:15.71,1:00:22.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,I will also show you that I can have light coming from there and from there, and then I get very particular diffraction.
Dialogue: 0,1:00:22.33,1:00:25.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,And that's all the optimization that we're going to do.
Dialogue: 0,1:00:28.27,1:00:35.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,So here you see that a little bit for every point in my source plane in the illumination I now get a number.
Dialogue: 0,1:00:35.78,1:00:41.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,I got plane waves onto the mask that leads to diffraction.
Dialogue: 0,1:00:41.82,1:00:45.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,So the diffraction orders are captured by the objective lens.
Dialogue: 0,1:00:45.04,1:00:48.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the bigger this lens, the bigger these opening angles,
Dialogue: 0,1:00:48.40,1:00:56.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,the more of these diffraction patterns I capture, and the better will be the reconstruction of the image here on the image plane.
Dialogue: 0,1:00:56.00,1:00:58.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,That's basically Fourier optics.
Dialogue: 0,1:00:58.98,1:01:09.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,So let's look a little bit more at diffraction for those of you that are not totally familiar with that, Mr. Huygens already said, hey, when you have light hitting an opening,
Dialogue: 0,1:01:09.42,1:01:14.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,each point here starts to act as a source of spherical waves,
Dialogue: 0,1:01:14.90,1:01:26.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,and it's really the path optical path length differences that then create this pattern here, where in some directions I end up getting light, and in other directions I do not get light.
Dialogue: 0,1:01:26.00,1:01:28.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,And those are called the diffraction orders.
Dialogue: 0,1:01:28.90,1:01:37.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,In essence, if I look at my aperture, the mask, and I think of that as a repeating pattern.
Dialogue: 0,1:01:37.44,1:01:44.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,I can take the Fourier transform of that pattern, and every diffraction order encodes one of the Fourier components.
Dialogue: 0,1:01:44.88,1:01:47.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,And you can think of the lens as a filter.
Dialogue: 0,1:01:47.64,1:01:51.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,How many of the Fourier components can I capture that lead.
Dialogue: 0,1:01:51.70,1:01:54.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,Help me reconstruct the pattern of the mask.
Dialogue: 0,1:01:54.66,1:01:58.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now on the wafer.
Dialogue: 0,1:01:58.12,1:02:04.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,So in certain directions, look at this wave from this opening.
Dialogue: 0,1:02:04.32,1:02:09.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,This one here had just this one was one wavelength to here this one was two.
Dialogue: 0,1:02:09.82,1:02:10.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,This one was three.
Dialogue: 0,1:02:10.96,1:02:19.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,So in this particular direction, all of these waves are in phase and they contribute to positive interference.
Dialogue: 0,1:02:19.42,1:02:23.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,And that is called the Bragg condition.
Dialogue: 0,1:02:23.36,1:02:31.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,Each of these diffraction patterns I can now project here in the pupil plane for the lens as a particular spot.
Dialogue: 0,1:02:31.76,1:02:36.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,And when these diffraction patterns start to fall outside the aperture of the lens,
Dialogue: 0,1:02:36.96,1:02:45.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,the numerical aperture of the lens, then no longer does it contribute to the image formation at the wafer level.
Dialogue: 0,1:02:45.84,1:02:48.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,It basically looks like something like this.
Dialogue: 0,1:02:48.34,1:02:49.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,My zeroth order.
Dialogue: 0,1:02:49.48,1:02:52.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,The light that straight goes through these waves.
Dialogue: 0,1:02:52.88,1:02:55.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,They lead to no contrast here.
Dialogue: 0,1:02:55.58,1:03:06.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,The first order is a wave with the same frequency as the pattern, the second order is triple the frequency, et cetera.
Dialogue: 0,1:03:06.34,1:03:12.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,So that's how in the end, I reconstruct the image at the wafer plane.
Dialogue: 0,1:03:12.54,1:03:13.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,I'm going a little bit fast.
Dialogue: 0,1:03:13.96,1:03:26.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,So now Abe was the one that said so now my challenge is I know what I want as a wafer,
Dialogue: 0,1:03:26.02,1:03:29.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,as a light intensity on the wafer plane.
Dialogue: 0,1:03:29.46,1:03:37.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,My challenge is to optimize the source here so that for every source point, the waves hitting the mask
Dialogue: 0,1:03:37.57,1:03:48.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,with how they go through the lens and all the path length differences, how that all adds up to this spatial frequency on the mask.
Dialogue: 0,1:03:48.73,1:03:50.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,Let me show that to you.
Dialogue: 0,1:03:50.39,1:03:51.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,Much simpler.
Dialogue: 0,1:03:51.73,1:04:02.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,Here we have that one central point that I was talking about this large illumination disk hitting a mask, creating diffraction patterns.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:02.83,1:04:06.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,Each of these diffraction patterns are within the pupil plane of the lens,
Dialogue: 0,1:04:06.93,1:04:13.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,and therefore they contribute to this interference pattern on the wafer.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:13.21,1:04:16.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,What happens now when I start to reduce the pitch.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:19.05,1:04:23.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,You see that some of the diffraction now moves further out.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:23.41,1:04:26.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,The contrast in my image starts to reduce.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:26.45,1:04:31.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,And I'm now at a CD basically of 0.5 lambda over an A.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:31.87,1:04:36.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,If I now go any further with shrinking,
Dialogue: 0,1:04:36.71,1:04:44.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,suddenly those outer points no longer are captured by the lens, and I have 0 contrast left.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:44.31,1:04:46.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,There's no imaging.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:46.19,1:04:46.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,But wait a minute.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:46.97,1:04:49.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,I can do some tricks.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:49.35,1:04:54.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,What if instead of using that point source, if I use a whole disk.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:57.31,1:05:05.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now you see that right here at the edge, there is, again, a little bit of overlap between the zeroth order and the first order.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:05.23,1:05:14.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I start to see a little bit of the reconstruction of my image at the wafer plane.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:14.07,1:05:25.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,But the contrast is very low because there is a lot of light from the center that doesn't really contribute anything to the interference.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:25.07,1:05:27.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,So what if I could move that light.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:27.85,1:05:30.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,That would improve my contrast.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:30.66,1:05:34.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I go and use what we call annular illumination.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:34.70,1:05:37.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,Instead of the light coming out of that whole disk, I now see it.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:37.88,1:05:47.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,If I look from the mask back at the illumination, I would see it come from a ring and I get much better contrast.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:47.10,1:05:50.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, if I can do that, why not do even more.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:50.50,1:05:54.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,Because that light here at the edges is also not contributing.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:54.66,1:05:57.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is where the positive interference come from.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:57.56,1:06:00.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,So let me remove that also.
Dialogue: 0,1:06:00.34,1:06:02.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,And I get even better contrast.
Dialogue: 0,1:06:02.82,1:06:06.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now I have something called dipole illumination.
Dialogue: 0,1:06:06.78,1:06:12.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,And that is what a lot of the fancy stuff that is happening nowadays.
Dialogue: 0,1:06:12.74,1:06:19.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,We can basically create with our illumination arbitrary shapes for the illumination.
Dialogue: 0,1:06:19.36,1:06:25.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,And what is the best illumination that will for the set of patterns lead to the best reconstruction.
Dialogue: 0,1:06:29.48,1:06:31.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,How does that then work out.
Dialogue: 0,1:06:31.52,1:06:39.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,If I have a full chip design, I will pick a bunch of critical patterns, the ones that I care about most.
Dialogue: 0,1:06:39.92,1:06:42.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,For those, I will start doing this optimization.
Dialogue: 0,1:06:42.90,1:06:46.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,I have a forward model, I keep calculating,
Dialogue: 0,1:06:46.24,1:06:54.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,I can use inverse lithography and try to do a more direct calculation, but in the end, I will end up with an illumination.
Dialogue: 0,1:06:54.28,1:06:56.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,You can see that there at the top.
Dialogue: 0,1:06:56.40,1:07:00.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,And with that illumination I can.
Dialogue: 0,1:07:00.08,1:07:01.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah there you go.
Dialogue: 0,1:07:01.02,1:07:04.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,We start to improve, iterate on the illumination.
Dialogue: 0,1:07:04.74,1:07:10.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,And you can now start seeing that as I project I get a cleaner and cleaner image.
Dialogue: 0,1:07:10.04,1:07:18.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,Once I have the very best illumination, I can say, OK, now let me use with this illumination OPC to improve the whole mask.
Dialogue: 0,1:07:18.64,1:07:22.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,And if I did the original selection of the patterns that I optimized for.
Dialogue: 0,1:07:22.94,1:07:29.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, and those were really the most critical ones then this illumination will actually work for the whole chip.
Dialogue: 0,1:07:33.02,1:07:36.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,So how does that optimization algorithm work.
Dialogue: 0,1:07:36.94,1:07:45.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,Basically we tend to look at hey, I want to this is what the designer drew this square thing.
Dialogue: 0,1:07:45.90,1:07:48.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now we know that we can never print something square.
Dialogue: 0,1:07:48.84,1:07:51.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,There's always finite resolution.
Dialogue: 0,1:07:51.34,1:07:56.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,So if I would try to optimize directly to that square shape,
Dialogue: 0,1:07:56.30,1:08:02.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,my total cost function, which is all the errors added up, would be completely dominated by these corners.
Dialogue: 0,1:08:02.60,1:08:03.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,And that's artificial.
Dialogue: 0,1:08:03.96,1:08:09.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,So we say, theoretically the best that I can do is some target that I can draw here.
Dialogue: 0,1:08:09.74,1:08:19.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,Let me now try to get the mask and the illumination so that I get as close as possible to what I think is possible, and I want to do that for a range of conditions,
Dialogue: 0,1:08:19.96,1:08:25.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,not just for nominal, but also for a little bit more or less dose.
Dialogue: 0,1:08:25.29,1:08:27.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,Dose is never perfectly controlled.
Dialogue: 0,1:08:27.57,1:08:29.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,A little bit more.
Dialogue: 0,1:08:29.85,1:08:34.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,Some focus variation and the mask making is also not perfect.
Dialogue: 0,1:08:34.11,1:08:43.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I take all these variances into account and make sure that I arrive at something that is robust under these variations.
Dialogue: 0,1:08:43.57,1:08:46.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,Here are some of the masks that I will skip.
Dialogue: 0,1:08:46.33,1:08:51.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,Here are some of the math that I will also skip that determines how all of that works.
Dialogue: 0,1:08:51.63,1:09:02.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,But what I wanted to point out here is there are actually two terms here that is this here that captures some of the effects from non chemically amplified resist.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:02.85,1:09:07.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,And I want right now to focus your attention here for a moment at this term.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:07.97,1:09:10.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is a mask 3D effect.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:10.21,1:09:13.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,So what is a mask 3D effect.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:13.61,1:09:17.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,I'll go fast I'm nearly done.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:17.45,1:09:20.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,Here is one of those poles of the illumination.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:20.19,1:09:22.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,The zeroth order is light that goes through.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:22.81,1:09:24.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,It goes to my wafer.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:24.91,1:09:28.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,Here is the first diffraction order and it goes here.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:28.87,1:09:36.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, because of electromagnetic effects here, there is an extra phase difference between these two.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:36.11,1:09:38.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,That's called a mask 3D effect.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:38.25,1:09:47.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,And that means that instead of a really good reconstruction of all my Fourier components, imagine that I have a Fourier transform.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:47.13,1:09:57.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,But I'm a little bit off on the amplitude of some of these components, or I'm a little bit off on the phase of these components that would start to affect your reconstruction.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:57.23,1:10:01.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,So our goal is to reconstruct how can I do that correctly.
Dialogue: 0,1:10:01.53,1:10:04.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,Because my one pole remember the dipole illumination.
Dialogue: 0,1:10:04.91,1:10:06.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,This was one pole.
Dialogue: 0,1:10:06.31,1:10:11.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,And as a result of that phase difference you actually get a shift in your mask.
Dialogue: 0,1:10:11.37,1:10:16.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,You see this phi minus phi be that phase difference leads to a shift in your mask.
Dialogue: 0,1:10:16.49,1:10:23.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,Instead of the intensity being focused around the ideal position, it has shifted a little bit.
Dialogue: 0,1:10:23.18,1:10:27.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now the other pole will have this shift in the other direction.
Dialogue: 0,1:10:27.22,1:10:34.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,So my total image is now the sum of two distributions.
Dialogue: 0,1:10:34.02,1:10:38.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,If they were perfectly aligned I would have a nice sharp image, but they are not.
Dialogue: 0,1:10:38.64,1:10:43.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,So the image fades I get a reduction in contrast.
Dialogue: 0,1:10:43.30,1:10:44.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,What could I do.
Dialogue: 0,1:10:44.54,1:10:47.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,Could I do something smart.
Dialogue: 0,1:10:47.02,1:10:56.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,What if I have the illumination and instead of the dipole here, I move this pole down a little bit.
Dialogue: 0,1:10:56.12,1:11:03.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,I move this pole up a little bit, and in my lens I can now compensate for the mask's 3D effects.
Dialogue: 0,1:11:03.26,1:11:12.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,Because this order here, these diffraction orders here and here are the zeroth order.
Dialogue: 0,1:11:12.68,1:11:19.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,And now I can have the one pole shift to the left, the other one a little bit to the right.
Dialogue: 0,1:11:19.72,1:11:22.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,And now they overlap again perfectly.
Dialogue: 0,1:11:22.88,1:11:24.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now that's simple.
Dialogue: 0,1:11:24.56,1:11:29.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,If I only have to do with a 2D pattern, what we can now do.
Dialogue: 0,1:11:29.14,1:11:33.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,And that's why we have computers, I can actually generalize this.
Dialogue: 0,1:11:33.34,1:11:36.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,And I can take an arbitrary mask pattern for logic.
Dialogue: 0,1:11:36.72,1:11:48.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,And I can say, hey, what would now be the very best aberration pattern in my lens that would make that all these images from these poles overlap as well as possible.
Dialogue: 0,1:11:48.92,1:11:50.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,And that's what you see here.
Dialogue: 0,1:11:50.80,1:11:58.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,Here is what I just explained for a simple if all I look at is lens is lines and spaces.
Dialogue: 0,1:11:58.24,1:12:02.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is for the actual mask pattern here.
Dialogue: 0,1:12:02.72,1:12:10.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,And as a result, I get much better exposure, latitude and much better adaptive focus in my final imaging.
Dialogue: 0,1:12:10.08,1:12:18.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I hope that with that, I've given you a little bit of a flavor on how we can use computation to optimize the settings on the scanner,
Dialogue: 0,1:12:18.68,1:12:23.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,both from an illumination perspective as well as aberrations in the lens.
Dialogue: 0,1:12:23.14,1:12:31.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,We try to minimize the aberrations, and sometimes we introduce them on purpose to correct for mass 3D effects.
Dialogue: 0,1:12:31.34,1:12:35.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now, with all of that said, let me finish up the last part.
Dialogue: 0,1:12:35.06,1:12:39.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,Just a slide on the roadmap and the outlook.
Dialogue: 0,1:12:39.42,1:12:45.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,Remember the best possible resolution at the lowest possible price.
Dialogue: 0,1:12:45.06,1:12:50.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,So let's look at we've looked at the best possible resolution.
Dialogue: 0,1:12:50.20,1:12:55.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,Reduced the lambda, increase DNA decrease K1.
Dialogue: 0,1:12:55.54,1:13:04.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,Let's now look for a moment that cost of the technology the productivity the EUV light source.
Dialogue: 0,1:13:04.38,1:13:13.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,We now we've recently announced that our latest results in San Diego, we can get one kilowatt of power out of our dose.
Dialogue: 0,1:13:13.68,1:13:19.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,And remember what Tony said in the beginning we struggled to get one Watt.
Dialogue: 0,1:13:19.97,1:13:24.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,Then at some point we were really excited that we got 10 watts.
Dialogue: 0,1:13:24.17,1:13:29.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,TSMC told us that when we hit 80w, we can go into volume production.
Dialogue: 0,1:13:29.99,1:13:32.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,That was in 2019.
Dialogue: 0,1:13:32.69,1:13:38.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,That was 2 watts or that was 281 was 2014 was 214.
Dialogue: 0,1:13:38.47,1:13:39.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK yeah.
Dialogue: 0,1:13:39.43,1:13:44.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,Sorry so that's when they said, OK, now we are serious about it.
Dialogue: 0,1:13:44.31,1:13:46.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,We think that you can get there.
Dialogue: 0,1:13:46.09,1:13:49.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,And by 2019 we were at 250.
Dialogue: 0,1:13:49.97,1:13:57.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,We now have in our hands demonstrations that we can actually hit 1 kilowatt.
Dialogue: 0,1:13:57.61,1:14:05.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,Another way of reducing the cost of the system is by having more commonality between EUV.
Dialogue: 0,1:14:05.09,1:14:11.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,Sorry, between the .33 and a system and the .55 and a system.
Dialogue: 0,1:14:11.69,1:14:22.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,So we have a roadmap on how we can create a more common platform that we will do that with also an option to in the future,
Dialogue: 0,1:14:22.61,1:14:31.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,start looking at 0.75, for which we already have some prototype designs and where we can get at the end.
Dialogue: 0,1:14:31.95,1:14:43.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,Or here is an example of with 13.5 nanometer EUV light from Paul scherrer Institute interferometric EUV lithography, where you do not have mass 3D effects.
Dialogue: 0,1:14:43.61,1:14:48.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,If we could really control everything can see that.
Dialogue: 0,1:14:48.03,1:14:57.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah, there are already some evidence that half pitches of about five or six nanometer as a absolute theoretical maximum would be possible.
Dialogue: 0,1:14:57.59,1:15:04.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,So we still have quite a ways to go that we can scale more than where we are today.
Dialogue: 0,1:15:04.67,1:15:10.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,And with that, I come to the end, and I hope that I fulfilled my promise that I wouldn't be too long.
Dialogue: 0,1:15:13.47,1:15:15.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,Tony, why don't you join me for questions.
Dialogue: 0,1:15:18.70,1:15:21.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,Hello what happened here.
Dialogue: 0,1:15:21.22,1:15:22.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,No it's fine.
Dialogue: 0,1:15:22.22,1:15:25.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,He was supposed to activate from that.
Dialogue: 0,1:15:25.54,1:15:26.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,All right.
Dialogue: 0,1:15:26.42,1:15:27.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,Oh, I think it's on now.
Dialogue: 0,1:15:27.62,1:15:34.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK, so we'll take questions now for both speakers, and we're open for questions.
Dialogue: 0,1:15:38.54,1:15:42.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,Do I understand correctly that if the end user cares about,
Dialogue: 0,1:15:42.80,1:15:48.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,let's say, resolution just along one axis, that's an easier problem from your perspective.
Dialogue: 0,1:15:48.43,1:15:54.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,Is that correct to say, is that something that the end users are trying to or you all collectively are trying to leverage.
Dialogue: 0,1:15:54.84,1:15:58.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK, so let's define first what the end user is, right.
Dialogue: 0,1:15:58.18,1:16:08.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,If the end user is Apple then they tell TSMC it's your problem.
Dialogue: 0,1:16:08.14,1:16:17.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,TSMC will tell us, hey, do you we want to use dipole illumination combination, because that gives the very best contrast for one dimensional patterns.
Dialogue: 0,1:16:17.92,1:16:25.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,Can you make a light source where all of the light only comes from these two points in that whole disk.
Dialogue: 0,1:16:25.36,1:16:35.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, we can do that pretty well, but if you make these spots too small, we can only do that by throwing some of the light away.
Dialogue: 0,1:16:35.92,1:16:40.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,And if we throw some of the light away, then it goes at the expense of productivity.
Dialogue: 0,1:16:40.52,1:16:48.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so our newer illumination designs can use a much smaller, what we call a pupil fill ratio.
Dialogue: 0,1:16:48.90,1:16:54.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,What's the smallest fraction of that whole illumination disk that you can define.
Dialogue: 0,1:16:54.70,1:17:00.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,That's where I want the light to come from and still use 100% of the light.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:00.04,1:17:06.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,So it's really a shared responsibility between us and the customer.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:06.44,1:17:13.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,But for every layer metal 0, metal one, metal 2 contact holes, vias.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:13.55,1:17:16.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,Every layer has its own illumination.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:16.59,1:17:20.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,And let me take your example a little bit to an extreme.
